{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"The board should ask management to investigate:\n\n- **Comparable valuations:** Does the closing ETF price reflect a trade, quote midpoint or auction? Is NAV contemporaneous, with consistent market hours, corporate actions and fair-value treatment? Recalculate apparent gaps using synchronized prices.\n- **Peers:** Do comparable ETFs show similar deviations after accounting for benchmarks, spreads and valuation methods?\n- **Underlying frictions:** Examine constituent liquidity, trading halts, spreads, short availability, hedging costs, settlement constraints, basket composition and creation/redemption fees.\n- **Arbitrage participation:** Assess actual AP activity—not merely signed agreements—creation/redemption flows, rejected orders, dealer capacity, market-maker quote quality and concentration. Ask participants whether apparent opportunities remain profitable after costs.\n\nDepending on findings, consider valuation or data corrections, basket/fee/process changes, additional APs or market makers, and exchange discussions about closing liquidity. Monitor persistence, executable deviations and investor costs.\n\n**Worth knowing:** A recurring closing gap is evidence to investigate, not proof of arbitrage failure or an automatic numerical trigger."}}
